1. SELECT * and Why to Avoid It 
SELECT * expands to ALL columns in definition order
Three reasons it's bad for production:
Schema changes break code silently — new columns appear in output, breaking downstream code expecting fixed columns
Slower queries — fetches unnecessary columns, wastes disk I/O, memory, network
Cannot use a covering index — no index contains ALL columns, so PostgreSQL must always touch the heap
SELECT * is acceptable ONLY for quick exploration in pgAdmin (peeking at a new table)
COUNT(*) is DIFFERENT — the * inside COUNT means "count all rows," does NOT fetch all columns
SELECT *, city FROM customers; is valid — returns all columns PLUS city again (city appears twice)
Right now, SELECT * and explicit column list return IDENTICAL results. The difference only manifests when schema changes in the future.

2. Column Aliasing with AS 
AS renames a column only in the query result, NOT in the actual table
AS is optional in PostgreSQL but should ALWAYS be written explicitly for readability
Without AS: SELECT id name FROM customers; → name becomes an alias for id (missing-comma alias trap!)
Alias with spaces requires double quotes: AS "Customer Name"
Double-quoted aliases are case-sensitive: "Customer Name" ≠ "customer name"
Best practice: use lowercase_with_underscores for aliases, avoid spaces entirely
Alias in WHERE: NOT allowed (WHERE executes before SELECT)
Alias in ORDER BY: Allowed (ORDER BY executes after SELECT)
Reserved words as aliases require quoting: AS "select" — but don't do this, use non-reserved names
You can alias a column to the same name as another column: SELECT name AS city — valid but confusing

3. DISTINCT — Removing Duplicate Rows 
SELECT DISTINCT city FROM customers; returns only unique city values
DISTINCT must come immediately after SELECT, before the column list
DISTINCT only affects the query result — the actual table is unchanged
DISTINCT on multiple columns deduplicates based on the COMBINATION of all listed columns
SELECT DISTINCT city, name → unique (city, name) pairs, not unique cities AND unique names separately
DISTINCT treats all NULLs as equal for deduplication — multiple NULLs collapse into one NULL row
SELECT DISTINCT * considers ALL columns together — if any column differs, rows are distinct
COUNT(DISTINCT column) returns the NUMBER of unique values (a single number)
SELECT DISTINCT column returns the actual unique VALUES (one row per unique value)
DISTINCT is universal across all SQL databases — same syntax and behavior everywhere
